msgid "Micrometer Observation API"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"The https://docs.micrometer.io/micrometer/reference/observation/introduction.html[Micrometer Observation API] provides a single instrumentation point that produces both traces and metrics.\n"
"Instead of separately instrumenting your code with a `Tracer` for traces and a `MeterRegistry` for metrics, you create an `Observation` that automatically generates both signals."
msgstr ""

msgid "This document is part of the xref:observability.adoc[Observability in Quarkus reference guide] which features this and other observability related components."
msgstr "このドキュメントは、これや他のオブザーバビリティー関連コンポーネントを特徴とする xref:observability.adoc[Quarkus におけるオブザーバビリティーリファレンスガイド] の一部です。"

msgid "The `quarkus-observation` extension integrates the Observation API with Quarkus, providing:"
msgstr ""

msgid "The `@Observed` CDI interceptor for declarative instrumentation"
msgstr ""

msgid "Programmatic `Observation` API for manual instrumentation"
msgstr ""

msgid "Observations produce traces and a Timer metric"
msgstr ""

msgid "Automatic bridging to OpenTelemetry tracing (when `quarkus-opentelemetry` is present)"
msgstr ""

msgid "Automatic bridging to Micrometer metrics (via any configured `MeterRegistry`)"
msgstr ""

msgid "Context propagation across threads and async boundaries"
msgstr ""

msgid "Using the extension"
msgstr "エクステンションの使用"

msgid ""
"If you already have your Quarkus project, you can add the `quarkus-observation` extension\n"
"to it by running the following command in your project base directory:"
msgstr ""

msgid "This will add the following to your build file:"
msgstr "これにより、以下がビルドファイルに追加されます。"

msgid "Choosing backends"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"The `quarkus-observation` extension produces traces and metrics through pluggable backends.\n"
"Add the backend extensions that match your infrastructure:"
msgstr ""

msgid "Scenario"
msgstr "シナリオ"

msgid "Additional dependencies"
msgstr ""

msgid "What you get"
msgstr ""

msgid "Observation API only"
msgstr ""

msgid "None"
msgstr "なし"

msgid "`@Observed` interceptor and programmatic API work. Metrics not sent anywhere because a metrics registry implementation is needed. No Traces produced."
msgstr ""

msgid "OpenTelemetry tracing"
msgstr ""

msgid "`quarkus-opentelemetry`"
msgstr ""

msgid "Observations produce OTel spans with parent-child relationships and context propagation"
msgstr ""

msgid "Prometheus metrics"
msgstr ""

msgid "`quarkus-micrometer-registry-prometheus`"
msgstr ""

msgid "Observation timer metrics scraped at `/q/metrics` in Prometheus format"
msgstr ""

msgid "OTel tracing + Prometheus metrics"
msgstr ""

msgid "`quarkus-opentelemetry` + `quarkus-micrometer-registry-prometheus`"
msgstr ""

msgid "Spans via OTel exporters, metrics via Prometheus scraping"
msgstr ""

msgid "Full OTel backend (traces + metrics)"
msgstr ""

msgid "`quarkus-micrometer-opentelemetry`"
msgstr ""

msgid "Spans and metrics exported via OTel OTLP protocol"
msgstr ""

msgid "No telemetry will be produce without a backend. The `quarkus-observation` extension instruments the code, but it doesn't have the ability to send data out of your app."
msgstr ""

msgid "For example, to get OpenTelemetry tracing with Prometheus metrics from observations the following dependencies are needed:"
msgstr ""

msgid "Using <code>@Observed</code>"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"The `@Observed` annotation instruments CDI bean methods declaratively.\n"
"Each invocation creates an `Observation` that produces a trace span and a timer metric."
msgstr ""

msgid "Creates an observation named after the method (`greet`). The span contextual name defaults to `GreetingService#greet`."
msgstr ""

msgid "Overrides the observation name (used for the timer metric name)."
msgstr ""

msgid "Overrides the contextual name (used for the span name)."
msgstr ""

msgid "Adds low-cardinality key-values as tags on both the span and the metric."
msgstr ""

msgid "Supported return types"
msgstr "サポートされている戻り値のタイプ"

msgid "The `@Observed` interceptor handles synchronous and asynchronous return types:"
msgstr ""

msgid "Synchronous methods — the observation spans the method execution"
msgstr ""

msgid "`Uni<T>` — the observation stays open until the `Uni` completes or fails"
msgstr ""

msgid "`Multi<T>` — the observation stays open until the `Multi` completes, fails, or is cancelled"
msgstr ""

msgid "`CompletionStage<T>` — the observation stays open until the future completes"
msgstr ""

msgid "Automatic attributes"
msgstr ""

msgid "Every `@Observed` method automatically gets these low-cardinality key-values (which become span attributes and metric tags):"
msgstr ""

msgid "`code.function` — the method name"
msgstr ""

msgid "`code.namespace` — the fully qualified class name"
msgstr ""

msgid "Programmatic observations"
msgstr ""

msgid "For finer control, inject `ObservationRegistry` and create observations manually."
msgstr ""

msgid "Simple observation"
msgstr ""

msgid "The `observe()` method handles start, scope, stop, and error recording automatically."
msgstr ""

msgid "Nested observations with scopes"
msgstr ""

msgid "Observations created within a scope automatically become children of the outer observation:"
msgstr ""

msgid "Error recording"
msgstr ""

msgid "Errors are recorded on the observation and propagated to the span (as error status) and metric (as an `error` tag):"
msgstr ""

msgid "Records the error on the span and adds `error=<exception class>` to the metric tags."
msgstr ""

msgid "Low and high cardinality attributes"
msgstr ""

msgid "Low and high cardinality attributes will end up in traces but only low cardinality ones will be used as metrics Tags (or attributes, if OTel is used)."
msgstr ""

msgid "Cardinality is related with the nr. of different values an attribute can have."
msgstr ""

msgid "As an example, the deployment.environment.name attribute typically has a small, fixed set of possible values such as test, development, staging, and prod. This results in a cardinality of 4, which is considered *low*."
msgstr ""

msgid "In contrast, a user identifier represented by enduser.id in a system with 2 million users can have up to 2 million distinct values. This leads to a cardinality of 2 million, which is considered *high*."
msgstr ""

msgid "All frameworks face challenges with high cardinality, but the Observation API offers a clean interface that encourages you to consider this issue upfront and keep it under control."
msgstr ""

msgid "It's up to the user to understand if an attribute is low or high cardinality and place it under the right bucket."
msgstr ""

msgid "High-cardinality attributes in metrics can cause a rapid increase in the number of metric dimensions, potentially leading to excessive memory usage and out-of-memory errors."
msgstr ""

msgid "Sender and Receiver contexts"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"For distributed trace propagation across service boundaries, use `SenderContext` and `ReceiverContext`.\n"
"These contexts tell the tracing handler to inject or extract trace context from a carrier (e.g., HTTP headers, message headers)."
msgstr ""

msgid "Sender (outgoing requests)"
msgstr ""

msgid "A setter function that injects trace context keys into the carrier."
msgstr ""

msgid "`Kind.CLIENT` creates a span with `SpanKind.CLIENT`. Use `Kind.PRODUCER` for messaging."
msgstr ""

msgid "Receiver (incoming requests)"
msgstr ""

msgid "A getter function that extracts trace context keys from the carrier."
msgstr ""

msgid "`Kind.SERVER` creates a span with `SpanKind.SERVER`. Use `Kind.CONSUMER` for messaging."
msgstr ""

msgid "Customization"
msgstr "カスタマイズ"

msgid ""
"The Observation API provides several CDI extension points for customizing behavior.\n"
"Register them as CDI beans and they are automatically discovered."
msgstr ""

msgid "ObservationFilter"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"An `ObservationFilter` modifies observation contexts before they are processed by handlers.\n"
"Use it to add common tags to all observations:"
msgstr ""

msgid "ObservationPredicate"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"An `ObservationPredicate` controls which observations are created.\n"
"Return `false` to suppress an observation entirely (no span, no metric):"
msgstr ""

msgid "ObservedInterceptorConvention"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"An `ObservedInterceptorConvention` customizes the naming and tagging for `@Observed` methods.\n"
"It overrides the default convention for all intercepted methods:"
msgstr ""

msgid "Custom MeterObservationHandler"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"A custom `MeterObservationHandler` replaces the default metric recording logic.\n"
"When provided as a CDI bean, it takes over from the built-in `DefaultMeterObservationHandler` supported by Micrometer. This is an example using Micrometer's MeterRegistry:"
msgstr ""

msgid "When using the `quarkus-micrometer-opentelemetry` bridge, the metrics will be implemented with OpenTelemetry."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"A custom `MeterObservationHandler` CDI bean replaces the default handler entirely.\n"
"The default handler creates timers for each observation. If you provide a custom handler, you are responsible for all metric recording."
msgstr ""

msgid "Disabling the extension"
msgstr ""

msgid "To disable the Observation extension at build time:"
msgstr ""

msgid "When disabled, `@Observed` interceptors are not registered and no `ObservationRegistry` bean is created."
msgstr ""

